  = @pg_title
  &#8221;

:markdown
  I spent most of my early life in the north of England; the home of
  the industrial revolution.

  I used to paint landscapes; it's hard not to be inspired by the moors
  and peaks of north Derbyshire. Though that style of painting was
  fun, and taught me much about technique and the handling of paint, I
  found it intellectually unfulfilling, and drifted towards to the more
  'academic' work I produce now.

  These were the last landscape pieces I made. They are intaglio prints,
  with the plates made with cardboard and duct tape, and they represent
  the hills of the North as the smoke, rust, and dye they gave to the
  world. Each picture has two panels: in the second panel the hills are
  cut through with roads.

